A||||8||mrfiero||inner tie rod removal?||||5||
Z||000000||mrfiero||07-09-2001||05:54 PM||||I have a loose (out of the car) '88 rack that I am "rebulding"......new bushings and inner & outer tie rods.  I loosened the bellow and popped out the bushing, but I can't figure out how the inner tie rod comes off.  <P>Any help would be appreciated......I'm not in a hurry, but wanted to get started on it.<P>Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||West Coast Fiero||07-09-2001||06:08 PM|||| I have found that the best tool for this is to use a fairly large pair of channel locks for removal and installation. Simply get a good hold of the tie rod and spin off<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||07-09-2001||06:17 PM||||OK.....the outer one spun off (I removed it already), but the inner seems to be pressed on or something.  I tried whacking it with a BFH, but it did no good.  I even heated it up with a propane torch, but it won't budge.<P>I'll go look at it again.<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||07-09-2001||06:27 PM||||I believe I used an adjustable wrench, a pipe wrench and a couple of pieces of chain link fence posts for leverage. The tie rod end is "crimped" on (can't remember the technical term). There should be flat surfaces machined on the tie rod end and on the rack for gripping. It takes a lot of force to uncrimp it. Also, I think there is a white plastic piece you have to slide off first. Look closely, you should see what I'm talking about.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||PAUL88GT||07-09-2001||06:35 PM||||mrfiero:<P>I just changed one on my 86se restoration project.  I used vise grips.  There are two flat spaces that have been machined into the tie rod end on the rack end.  It is threaded onto a stud.  It was on tight, but I was able to break is loose by hand and I am not that strong.  Hope this helps.  A large pair of channel locks would probably work also.<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||07-09-2001||07:04 PM||||There are two ways of doing this.  You can use channel locks, wrench etc... when i did mine i had to drop my rack because i didn't have right angle to grip the inner rod, bad luck.<P>2nd you can go to your parts store, and get a inner tie rod socket, its about 1-2 feet long.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||07-09-2001||09:06 PM||||Success!  Thanks for everyones input.  I have done just about everything (repairwise) to a Fiero, except the rack, but I have never had to replace the bushings or tie rods, so this was all new to me.  The inners definitely screw on & off......I just had to put more muscle into it.<P>Thanks again......now I just need to wait for the inners from Rodney to finish it up.<P>BTW.....I can't imagine trying to do that with the rack still on the car.  To those of you who have done that kudos to you!   <P>Luckily I have enough spare parts lying around to repair/replace things with no down time to my car......at least until I install them (then it's just a little R&R).<BR>   <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 07-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||West Coast Fiero||07-09-2001||09:48 PM||||you did put red lock-tite on the threads for the inner tie rods right?||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||07-09-2001||10:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>you did put red lock-tite on the threads for the inner tie rods right?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Nope!   I haven't gotten the replacement inner tie rods yet.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I will use it when I put it back together.....thanks. <BR> ||||reg||1
